Roger Harkavy wrote:
> He is expert in dagger and I keep a dozen orcish daggers quivered.
> What is the best dagger weapon you can have? Does the type matter
> (elvish/orcish/normal)?
	The type does matter - do note that the unidentified name for orcish 
daggers are "crude" daggers. Of the three you mention, the elven daggers 
are the best and the orcish daggers are the worst. Silver daggers are 
probably better than the elven daggers again(because of the "magical" 
properties of silver), but they are very rare, so you probably
shouldn't bother with them.
	I'd advise you to get normal or elven daggers instead of your orcish, 
though; and when you do get a nice stack, you should make them rustproof 
and enchant them as high as you safely can - daggers are a great weapon 
for a rogue.

> Is there a way to poison the daggers?
	No, there isn't, for balance reasons - AFAIK only missiles that may 
disappear as they hit their target can be poisoned.

> Right now he's only skilled in knife, but as he uses the crysknife
> more he should eventually master it. Should I stick with this as a
> weapon?
	Sadly, I don't think investing points in the knife skill was a wise 
move - there aren't any knife artifacts, and you probably won't want to 
have a non-artifact as your primary weapon. The crysknife seems to be a 
nice weapon, but I wouldn't master it, at least not until you
feel that you have all the skills you really need(i.e. expert in 
daggers, and as high as it gets in a good weapon skill like long sword
or saber - if you're going to #twoweapon, you'll need skill points for
that, and perhaps a secondary weapon as well).

> Also, the gauntlets are a little corroded. How do I fix this?
	Rust- and fireproofing are useful "alternate functions" of the
scrolls you'd normally use to enchant your armor and weapons. You have
to somehow get your character to mispronounce the words on the scroll
to get the effect you desire.
